# Hypothesis (1985 wealth, ever-incarcerated respondents, NLSY79 extract n=12,013)

Among respondents ever flagged as incarcerated (`ever_jailed` = 1, jailed 1985-1994, n=289 = 2.4%),
1985 median wealth (`composite_wealth_1985`) is near the floor of the distribution for everyone -
$720 vs $3,600 for never-jailed respondents (20% of the unincarcerated level) with 39.4% holding
exactly $0 - and the disparity that organises it is **first incarceration status, then sex, and only
weakly race**. Within race x sex cells, later jail is associated with a median wealth shortfall of
$4,300 among Hispanic men ($700 vs $5,000; ratio 0.14; MWU p=0.00012, Cliff's delta -0.32) and
$3,500 among white men ($2,000 vs $5,500; ratio 0.36; p=0.029, delta -0.17), but only $700 among
Black men ($800 vs $1,500; ratio 0.53; p=0.082, delta -0.09), whose CI touches zero. Because
never-jailed Black men are already at $1,500 - below even the *jailed* white men at $2,000 - the
absolute floor reached by incarcerated respondents of every race ($700-$2,000) is compressed enough
that race ordering *within* the incarcerated group is not resolvable (Black vs white jailed men
-$1,200, 95% CI -$4,680 to +$600, p=0.15; Hispanic vs white -$1,300, CI -$5,000 to +$1,400, p=0.19;
Black vs Hispanic +$100, p=0.87), and the one race contrast that is nominally "significant" inside
the incarcerated group is on the sex axis: pooled jailed women (n=37) have median $0 vs $1,000 for
pooled jailed men (gap -$1,000, CI -$1,400 to -$260, p=0.024, delta -0.22). That sex gap is driven
by zero-dominance rather than by debt: 64.9% of jailed women report exactly $0, rising to 81.2% of
Black jailed women (n=16) and 77.8% of Hispanic jailed women (n=9) - cells whose medians are
structurally pinned at 0 and whose magnitudes must not be read as point estimates. The incarceration
shortfall is not a bottom-only artifact: median deficits widen monotonically up the distribution
(p25 $0, p50 -$2,880, p75 -$10,100, p90 -$49,610) while relative ratios stay roughly flat (0.20-0.37),
i.e. incarceration tracks a multiplicative compression of wealth, and it widens after 1985
(overall gap -$2,880 in 1985, -$10,500 in 1990, -$8,500 in 1996, with jailed medians at $0 in every
race cell by 1990). This is a prospective association - 1985 wealth is measured at or before most
jail spells, so the correct reading is that low 1985 wealth is a *precursor* marker of later jail,
not its effect - and with self-reported jail spells (which understate exposure and bias toward the
null) and no weights, covariates or residence/group-quarters field to identify incarceration any
other way, magnitudes should be treated as lower bounds on a descriptive pattern.
